[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR BGP ECMP configuration on the leaf routers. Mechanism: Each leaf learns two equal-cost eBGP paths to the opposite rack’s subnet via both spines, but only a single “best” path is being installed in the RIB due to standard BGP tie-break (lowest router-id), so all east–west traffic funnels through one spine. Evidence: On leaf_router_0_0, “show ip bgp 10.0.1.0/24” shows two paths via 172.16.0.4 (AS 65100) and 172.16.0.8 (AS 65101), but “show ip route 10.0.1.0/24” installs only 172.16.0.4. On leaf_router_0_1, “show ip bgp 10.0.0.0/24” shows two paths via 172.16.0.6 and 172.16.0.10, yet “show ip route 10.0.0.0/24” installs only 172.16.0.6. Both leaf FRR configs lack multipath knobs. Localization: leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1, IPv4 unicast in the default VRF; bgpd process and its route installation into the kernel. Root cause: BGP multipath is not enabled (no maximum-paths and no “bestpath as-path multipath-relax”), so ECMP across the two spines is not realized. Corrective fix: On both leaves, enable eBGP ECMP by configuring bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax and, under address-family ipv4 unicast, maximum-paths 2 (or desired width). After applying, verify that “show ip route 10.0.1.0/24” (leaf 0_0) and “show ip route 10.0.0.0/24” (leaf 0_1) display two ECMP next-hops and traffic is balanced across both spines. Current reachability is intact but suboptimal. 
=== END ===